00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:01.865
PRIYANKA VERGARDIA:
Hey, it's Priyanka.

00:00:01.865 --> 00:00:04.620
So far, we've looked at how
Critter Junction launched

00:00:04.620 --> 00:00:07.380
and globally scaled an
app on Compute Engine.

00:00:07.380 --> 00:00:10.170
Today, let's learn how they
can make their social critter

00:00:10.170 --> 00:00:14.145
app more scalable by gracefully
replacing failed instances.

00:00:14.145 --> 00:00:17.370
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:22.080 --> 00:00:24.610
With their growing
daily active users,

00:00:24.610 --> 00:00:27.570
we help them set up
autoscaling and global load

00:00:27.570 --> 00:00:30.030
balancing to handle
globally distributed

00:00:30.030 --> 00:00:32.380
and constantly rising traffic.

00:00:32.380 --> 00:00:34.940
But to keep their users from
risking their daily game

00:00:34.940 --> 00:00:38.010
streaks, they need to make
sure their app is available all

00:00:38.010 --> 00:00:39.780
the time without interruptions.

00:00:39.780 --> 00:00:42.270
So today, let's focus
on helping them set up

00:00:42.270 --> 00:00:45.120
high availability, or HA.

00:00:45.120 --> 00:00:47.880
We know that managed instance
groups provide features

00:00:47.880 --> 00:00:51.630
such as autoscaling, regional
multiple zone deployments,

00:00:51.630 --> 00:00:53.660
autohealing, and auto updating.

00:00:53.660 --> 00:00:56.190
We covered the autoscaling
and regional deployments

00:00:56.190 --> 00:00:57.340
in the last episode.

00:00:57.340 --> 00:01:00.030
So check it out to learn
more about global scaling.

00:01:00.030 --> 00:01:03.360
Today, let's focus on
autohealing and auto updates.

00:01:03.360 --> 00:01:06.510
Autohealing helps proactively
identify and replace

00:01:06.510 --> 00:01:08.040
the unhealthy instances--

00:01:08.040 --> 00:01:09.780
the ones that are
not responding--

00:01:09.780 --> 00:01:11.460
with the healthy ones.

00:01:11.460 --> 00:01:14.610
And the auto update feature
helps update the instances

00:01:14.610 --> 00:01:16.750
without disrupting the service.

00:01:16.750 --> 00:01:19.080
Let's focus on
autohealing for a bit.

00:01:19.080 --> 00:01:21.270
First, we create a
health check, which

00:01:21.270 --> 00:01:24.760
not only detects whether the
machine is running or not,

00:01:24.760 --> 00:01:27.390
but also detects
application-specific issues

00:01:27.390 --> 00:01:30.390
such as freezing,
crashing, or overloading.

00:01:30.390 --> 00:01:32.610
If an instance is
deemed unhealthy,

00:01:32.610 --> 00:01:36.030
new instances are created by
the managed instance group.

00:01:36.030 --> 00:01:38.700
Let's see how autohealing
works in action.

00:01:38.700 --> 00:01:41.100
In Google Cloud Console,
I have the project

00:01:41.100 --> 00:01:43.260
from the previous
episode opened here.

00:01:43.260 --> 00:01:46.560
First, we will create a health
check in Compute engine.

00:01:46.560 --> 00:01:47.340
Give it a name.

00:01:47.340 --> 00:01:48.806
Protocol is HTTP.

00:01:48.806 --> 00:01:51.340
And let's say the
path is /health.

00:01:51.340 --> 00:01:54.150
It can be any path you
decide for your health check.

00:01:54.150 --> 00:01:57.660
In our demo app, we added
code that ensures that /health

00:01:57.660 --> 00:02:00.470
returns a 200 OK
response when healthy,

00:02:00.470 --> 00:02:04.460
and HTTP 500 internal
server error when unhealthy.

00:02:04.460 --> 00:02:08.160
Now for setting up health
criteria, set check interval

00:02:08.160 --> 00:02:11.190
to 10, which means
every 10 seconds

00:02:11.190 --> 00:02:13.800
the service will be
probed for health.

00:02:13.800 --> 00:02:16.920
Set timeout to
five, which means we

00:02:16.920 --> 00:02:21.320
wait for maximum five seconds
for a response to a probe.

00:02:21.320 --> 00:02:23.880
Set a healthy
threshold to two, which

00:02:23.880 --> 00:02:26.100
defines the number
of sequential probes

00:02:26.100 --> 00:02:28.200
that must succeed
for the instance

00:02:28.200 --> 00:02:30.090
to be considered healthy.

00:02:30.090 --> 00:02:32.310
And finally, set an
unhealthy threshold

00:02:32.310 --> 00:02:35.700
to three, which defines the
number of sequential probes

00:02:35.700 --> 00:02:39.780
that must fail for the instance
to be considered unhealthy.

00:02:39.780 --> 00:02:41.250
And then create.

00:02:41.250 --> 00:02:44.740
As a best practice, you want a
health check to be conservative

00:02:44.740 --> 00:02:49.470
so you don't preemptively
delete and recreate instances.

00:02:49.470 --> 00:02:51.660
Now, let's go to
our instance group

00:02:51.660 --> 00:02:55.470
we created in the last episode
and add a health check to it.

00:02:55.470 --> 00:02:59.310
Select the health check with
an initial delay of 90 seconds.

00:02:59.310 --> 00:03:01.020
Ideally, this
initial delay should

00:03:01.020 --> 00:03:02.910
be long enough for
the instance to be

00:03:02.910 --> 00:03:06.510
fully running and ready
to respond as healthy.

00:03:06.510 --> 00:03:10.260
Let's have some fun with this
and simulate failures now.

00:03:10.260 --> 00:03:12.360
For that, we go
to the VM instance

00:03:12.360 --> 00:03:16.310
and click on external IP
and make it unhealthy.

00:03:16.310 --> 00:03:18.750
Wait for the autohealer
to take action,

00:03:18.750 --> 00:03:21.810
and we will see that the green
check mark next to the instance

00:03:21.810 --> 00:03:26.280
turns into a spinner indicating
that the autohealer has started

00:03:26.280 --> 00:03:28.750
rebooting that instance.

00:03:28.750 --> 00:03:31.470
One of the other concerns
when it comes to HA

00:03:31.470 --> 00:03:33.840
is applying updates
to the instances

00:03:33.840 --> 00:03:36.060
without impacting the service.

00:03:36.060 --> 00:03:37.740
Managed instance
groups allow you

00:03:37.740 --> 00:03:40.770
to control the speed and
scope of the update rollout

00:03:40.770 --> 00:03:44.290
to minimize disruptions
to your applications.

00:03:44.290 --> 00:03:46.740
You can also perform
partial rollouts, which

00:03:46.740 --> 00:03:48.960
allows for canary testing.

00:03:48.960 --> 00:03:51.030
Let's see that in action now.

00:03:51.030 --> 00:03:54.670
On our instance group, we see
this rolling update button.

00:03:54.670 --> 00:03:55.860
Click on that.

00:03:55.860 --> 00:03:58.950
Word "rolling" means it
is for gradual updates.

00:03:58.950 --> 00:04:01.920
We will add a second
template for canary testing

00:04:01.920 --> 00:04:04.800
and select target size as 20%.

00:04:04.800 --> 00:04:07.770
This means we want to
send 20% of the traffic

00:04:07.770 --> 00:04:10.560
to the new instances
for canary testing.

00:04:10.560 --> 00:04:14.070
Now, update mode is
by default proactive,

00:04:14.070 --> 00:04:16.860
which means Compute
Engine actively schedules

00:04:16.860 --> 00:04:20.640
actions to apply the
requested updates to instances

00:04:20.640 --> 00:04:21.959
as necessary.

00:04:21.959 --> 00:04:25.020
In many cases, this often
means deleting and recreating

00:04:25.020 --> 00:04:26.880
instances proactively.

00:04:26.880 --> 00:04:30.090
You can choose to perform
an opportunistic update

00:04:30.090 --> 00:04:33.760
if a proactive update is
potentially too disruptive.

00:04:33.760 --> 00:04:37.350
An opportunistic update is
only applied when you manually

00:04:37.350 --> 00:04:40.680
initiate the update
on selected instances

00:04:40.680 --> 00:04:43.650
or when new instances are
created by the managed instance

00:04:43.650 --> 00:04:44.150
group.

00:04:44.150 --> 00:04:46.950
Max surge means how
many more instances

00:04:46.950 --> 00:04:49.980
are you willing to spin up
as a part of this update?

00:04:49.980 --> 00:04:52.170
The higher value here
speeds up the update,

00:04:52.170 --> 00:04:54.940
but costs more for
the new instances.

00:04:54.940 --> 00:04:57.780
So it is a trade-off
between cost and speed.

00:04:57.780 --> 00:05:00.650
Max unavailable
and min wait time--

00:05:00.650 --> 00:05:03.390
I will keep them as zero,
but these parameters

00:05:03.390 --> 00:05:06.240
are used to control how
disruptive the update is

00:05:06.240 --> 00:05:09.450
to your service and to control
the rate at which the update is

00:05:09.450 --> 00:05:10.210
deployed.

00:05:10.210 --> 00:05:13.380
Through our help setting up
two high availability features

00:05:13.380 --> 00:05:15.930
within managed instance
groups, Critter Junction

00:05:15.930 --> 00:05:19.110
has a much more resilient
architecture now.

00:05:19.110 --> 00:05:22.560
Autohealing proactively
defies unhealthy insurgencies

00:05:22.560 --> 00:05:23.580
and heals them.

00:05:23.580 --> 00:05:25.800
And auto update
updates instances

00:05:25.800 --> 00:05:27.810
without disrupting the service.

00:05:27.810 --> 00:05:29.790
Stay tuned to find
out what more is

00:05:29.790 --> 00:05:31.680
in store for Critter Junction.

00:05:31.680 --> 00:05:33.660
And remember, always
be architecting.

00:05:33.660 --> 00:05:37.010
